<h2 class="mt-14">Consent and purchaser suppression for Personalization</h2>
<p>Before launching personalization automation, record eligibility separately for each purpose and channel. A declared product preference or purchase event does not itself grant permission for email or SMS marketing. Recheck current suppression, order, inventory, and case state immediately before sending; an entry-time segment can become stale while a message waits in a queue.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Audience state</th><th>Required handling</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>No documented marketing eligibility</td><td>Hold optional personalization marketing pending review of the applicable basis and channel rules</td><td>Necessary order or service notices require their own purpose and sender; they do not authorize promotions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eligible, never purchased</td><td>Use declared interests with useful fallback content; test an offer only within a margin limit</td><td>A preference is a relevance signal, not evidence that a discount is needed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Active cart, no purchase</td><td>Check recovery eligibility and current cart state before each reminder</td><td>Browsing and cart activity alone do not establish marketing permission</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purchased recently</td><td>Exit recovery for the purchased cart; assess separately eligible care, editorial, and later offers by purpose</td><td>A purchase does not require a permanent ban on all communication or authorize every follow-up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Refund or return open</td><td>Pause conflicting optional offers while routing necessary case updates through the service owner</td><td>Resolution does not automatically restore marketing eligibility</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repeated non-engagement</td><td>Review delivery, reliable clicks, purchases, preferences, and a defined inactivity window before reducing frequency</td><td>Missing opens alone do not establish disinterest; retain opt-out and complaint suppression records</td></tr>
<tr><td>SMS eligibility present</td><td>Check SMS-specific purpose, opt-out, quiet-time, and frequency rules separately from email</td><td>One channel's permission does not authorize another</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wholesale or B2B account</td><td>Review account terms, recipient role, and applicable channel eligibility</td><td>An account label neither grants marketing permission nor proves a retail offer violates terms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Free or disposable email domain</td><td>Use address validation, delivery failures, and documented abuse signals rather than a blanket free-domain exclusion</td><td>A free mailbox is not evidence of invalid consent or a junk signup</td></tr>
<tr><td>Staff and test accounts</td><td>Route identified test profiles to controlled QA and exclude them from production metrics</td><td>Internal activity can distort relevance and conversion reporting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Competitor or researcher signals</td><td>Apply the same documented eligibility and preference rules</td><td>Inferred identity is not a substitute for a permission record</td></tr>
<tr><td>Legacy list without timestamps</td><td>Quarantine optional marketing while reviewing evidence; send repermission only where that request is itself permitted</td><td>Missing records cannot be repaired by an otherwise unauthorized marketing send</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="mt-12">Margin, app costs, and pricing for Personalization</h2>
<p>Attributed revenue is not profit for personalization. A self-funding personalization program survives a full cost model: subscription, contact or send overages, SMS credits, capture tooling, templates, agency time, and every point of personalization discount margin. For personalization, a stack at fifteen percent of attributed margin needs simplification first, optimization second.</p>
<p>Pricing changes frequently and varies by region, contact volume, and contract term, so check the official pricing pages of every shortlisted app and model an eighteen-month total that includes a peak season. For personalization, free plans always bind somewhere; identify whether contacts, sends, branching, or support caps your personalization motion first.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost component</th><th>What to model</th><th>Common failure</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Platform subscription</td><td>Personalization tier priced at realistic contact scale</td><td>Personalization tier bought for phantom growth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contact or send overages</td><td>Personalization growth pace versus plan ceilings</td><td>Personalization overage invoices after drops</td></tr>
<tr><td>SMS credits</td><td>Personalization opt-in share times texts per journey</td><td>Personalization SMS assumed as cheap email</td></tr>
<tr><td>Discount budget</td><td>Personalization code depth times likely redemption</td><td>personalization flows teaching shoppers to hold out</td></tr>
<tr><td>Creative and ops time</td><td>Personalization upkeep hours per operating week</td><td>personalization maintenance hours wished away</td></tr>
<tr><td>Migration and setup</td><td>personalization switch priced in imports and rebuild labor</td><td>Personalization audit trail gaps after moves</td></tr>
<tr><td>Support and success tiers</td><td>Personalization support tier for critical incidents</td><td>Peak-week personalization outage without coverage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third-party integrations</td><td>personalization companion tools and their fees</td><td>personalization side-tools eclipsing core cost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deliverability remediation</td><td>personalization inbox-health monitoring and cleanup</td><td>personalization sender damage costing more than tools</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
